FAQ_HEADING = "Frequently Asked Questions"
FAQS = [
    ("1. Do I need a referral to see an oral surgeon in Australia?", "Many private OMS practices request a written referral from a dentist or GP, but some accept self-referrals for straightforward wisdom tooth consultations. For hospital-based procedures, a referral is usually required for Medicare and health fund purposes. If you need a referral quickly, ask your general dentist — they can often produce one within a day. For more on what to expect, see our <a href=\"https://fascinatingdentistry.com.au/blog/wisdom-teeth-removal/\">wisdom teeth removal guide</a>. 2"),
    ("2. How much does oral surgery cost in Australia, and what will my health fund cover?", "You can expect a specialist consultation fee of $160–$300, surgical extraction costs of $300–$600 per tooth, wisdom teeth from $350–$2,000 depending on complexity, and implants from $2,500–$5,500. “Major dental” extras cover part of surgical extractions and wisdom teeth, while “hospital cover” applies to day-surgery or hospital procedures. Always get a written gap quote before proceeding. Compare options by reading our <a href=\"https://fascinatingdentistry.com.au/blog/dental-implants/\">dental implants explained</a> guide. 3"),
    ("3. Is oral surgery covered by Medicare?", "Generally no — Medicare does not cover routine dental extractions or wisdom tooth removal. However, Medicare rebates apply to certain medical/hospital-based procedures such as orthognathic jaw surgery and some complex pathology removals under specific MBS item numbers. Your surgeon will advise whether you can claim anything. If you need urgent care, visit our <a href=\"https://fascinatingdentistry.com.au/australia/emergency-dentist/\">emergency dentist guide</a>. 4"),
    ("4. What is the difference between a dentist, an oral surgeon and an oral and maxillofacial surgeon in Australia?", "A general dentist performs simple extractions. An “oral surgeon” in Australia is usually an oral and maxillofacial surgeon (OMS) — someone with both medical and dental degrees and 4–5 years of accredited specialist training. They handle complex extractions, implants, jaw surgery and facial trauma. Always check specialist registration with AHPRA. You can learn more about surgical extractions from our <a href=\"https://fascinatingdentistry.com.au/australia/tooth-extraction/\">tooth extraction service page</a>. 5"),
    ("5. Can I have oral surgery under general anaesthetic in Australia?", "Yes. General anaesthesia is used for complex, long or multiple surgical procedures and is delivered in an accredited day surgery or private hospital with an anaesthetist present. IV sedation is often sufficient for wisdom teeth — you remain conscious but deeply relaxed. Your surgeon will recommend the safest option. Read more in our <a href=\"https://fascinatingdentistry.com.au/blog/dental-sedation/\">sedation and anaesthesia guide</a>. 6"),
    ("6. How long does recovery take after oral surgery, and when can I return to work or school?", "Most people need 2–5 days off work or school for wisdom tooth surgery. Swelling peaks around 48 hours and settles over the next few days. You can usually return to normal activities after day 3–5, but avoid strenuous exercise for a week. Sutures dissolve or are removed at a 1–2 week review. If you are planning surgery, check our <a href=\"https://fascinatingdentistry.com.au/australia/sydney/\">Sydney oral surgery page</a> or <a href=\"https://fascinatingdentistry.com.au/australia/melbourne/\">Melbourne oral surgery page</a> for local options. 7"),
    ("7. What is dry socket, and how can I prevent it after tooth removal?", "Dry socket (alveolar osteitis) happens when the blood clot dislodges from the extraction site, exposing bone. It usually causes severe throbbing pain 2–4 days after surgery. To reduce the risk, do not use straws, avoid smoking, and do not spit forcefully for at least 48 hours. If you suspect dry socket, contact your surgeon — it is treatable. See our <a href=\"https://fascinatingdentistry.com.au/blog/dry-socket/\">dry socket prevention guide</a> for more detail. 8"),
    ("8. How do I choose the best oral surgeon in Australia?", "Check AHPRA specialist registration, look for FRACDS(OMS), read recent Google reviews, consider location and hospital links, and always arrange a consultation to feel confident in the surgeon. Request a written treatment plan and cost estimate before consenting. For further tips, read our <a href=\"https://fascinatingdentistry.com.au/australia/tooth-extraction/\">guide to choosing a tooth extraction provider</a>."),
]

